  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 251 AM MST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nRidging across the Western United States alongside a shortwave \ntrough digging southeastward across the Midwest is forecast to \ncreate northwesterly flow at 500-mb overhead Friday morning, if it \nis not already in place. This flow pattern would promote a modest 60-\n80 kt jet streak from the Pacific Northwest into the Northern \nRockies. Surface winds out of the northwest are likely in the \nafternoon in association with a surface high in the Intermountain \nWest and a surface low in South-Central Canada, both favorably \nlocated with respect to the upper pattern. These downsloping \nwinds support highs climbing on Friday, with the current \nforecast in the upper 50s and lower 60s. NBM guidance suggests \nthat these northwesterly winds could gust into the 20-30 MPH \nrange north of I-70 during the late morning and early afternoon\nhours, however overestimation biases would hint at the lower \nend of this range. Even so, relative humidities (RH) are not \nexpected to drop below 15% based on LREF probabilities, which \nwould keep the risk for fire weather conditions to a minimum. \n\nGuidance indicates a shortwave trough embedded in the northwesterly \nflow regime may impact the area late Friday night and Saturday, \nthough it is currently uncertain whether this feature will be \npresent, and if so, how strong it will be. When looking at LREF \ncontributors, ENS members seem to show this feature most \nconsistently, though the bottom 10% of GEFS and GEPS members also \nshow the shortwave. If this shortwave develops, precipitation \nappears likely. When looking at the LREF Grand Ensemble (all \nmembers), we see a 20-30% chance for greater than 0.1 inches of \nsnowfall in portions of Eastern Colorado Saturday. Much of this area \nhas over a 40% chance of low temperatures at or below freezing \nFriday night according to NBM guidance, with as high as a 75% chance \nin portions of Northern Yuma County. Even so, high temperatures on \nSaturday are forecast to rise above freezing even in the coldest \nscenarios, which would suggest a transition into rain if wintry \nprecipitation is able to occur at all Friday night or Saturday \nmorning. There is also the chance that this shortwave system is very \nweak or not present at all, which represents about 50% of the LREF \nGrand Ensemble solutions. In this case, drier conditions could be \nexpected with downsloping winds from the west that produce warmer \ntemperatures, similar to Friday's highs. Confidence is highest for \nthe latter solution (50-60%), while there is only about 10% \nconfidence in the solution with wintry precipitation. \n\nDeterministic guidance begins to diverge going into Saturday night \nand Sunday. Looking at ensembles, upper northwesterly flow is \nfavored to last as late as Sunday night before a bit more zonal flow \ndevelops Monday with another shortwave trough. NBM 24-hr \nprecipitation guidance suggests a 90% chance or greater for no \nprecipitation Sunday through the end of the forecast period Tuesday \nevening. This could change on Monday if the shortwave trough can \ndeepen a bit similar to the current 00Z deterministic GFS. Highs \ncontinue to be forecast in the upper 40s and 50s Sunday through \nTuesday, at or a little above average for this time of year. \nHowever, these highs may be highly variable depending on how the \natmosphere is modified by the northwesterly flow pattern earlier in \nthe period.",
